      ":question/id": "e1385b74-52ff-53cd-91df-70b0f0ad6473",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-90600",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}}{xy}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}+3{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+3)}{{x}^{2}v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({v}^{2}+3)}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{2}+3}{v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v+\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186087456,
    ":question/id": "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-2301",
    ":question/problem": "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
